thunderbolt: keep the TB3 host router awake in suspend-to-idle

Rebased onto v6.18 from omacom/omarchy-pkgs PR #596
(0664-thunderbolt-keep-tb3-host-router-awake-in-s2idle.patch, angellindo).

On Apple Alpine Ridge hosts driven by the software connection manager,
tb_switch_suspend() sets the LC sleep bit on the host router from the
NHI's suspend_noirq. The router then stops answering PCIe config
accesses before the PCI core reaches the controller's bridges, so the
core saves all-ones and every tb_cfg access on resume fails with
-ESHUTDOWN. In suspend-to-idle the controller stays powered, so the
sleep bit is not needed there. Leave the host router awake for
suspend-to-idle only; S3, hibernation, runtime PM, device routers and
USB4 routers keep the current path.

--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/switch.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/switch.c
@@ -15,6 +15,7 @@
 #include <linux/sizes.h>
 #include <linux/slab.h>
 #include <linux/string_helpers.h>
+#include <linux/suspend.h>
 
 #include "tb.h"
 
@@ -3654,10 +3655,26 @@
 
 	tb_switch_set_wake(sw, flags, runtime);
 
-	if (tb_switch_is_usb4(sw))
+	if (tb_switch_is_usb4(sw)) {
 		usb4_switch_set_sleep(sw);
-	else
-		tb_lc_set_sleep(sw);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	/*
+	 * The LC sleep bit prepares a Thunderbolt 3 router for power
+	 * removal. In suspend-to-idle the host router stays powered. On
+	 * Apple Alpine Ridge hosts setting the bit there makes the router
+	 * stop answering: its PCIe config space reads all-ones before the
+	 * PCI core saves it, and every tb_cfg access on resume fails with
+	 * -ESHUTDOWN. Leave the host router awake for suspend-to-idle only.
+	 */
+	if (!runtime && !tb_route(sw) &&
+	    pm_suspend_target_state == PM_SUSPEND_TO_IDLE) {
+		tb_sw_dbg(sw, "suspend-to-idle, not setting LC sleep\n");
+		return;
+	}
+
+	tb_lc_set_sleep(sw);
 }
 
